// Fixture: domain_logical_id_collision_error
// Rule under test: GDDL Spec v4 §4.1.1 -- logical ID collision detection. Two entries
// in the SAME identifier domain with different keys but IDENTICAL description text
// produce the same logical ID, since the entry's key is not part of the hash input
// (only `Domain::description text` is hashed, per §4.1.1). This is a GENUINE,
// DETERMINISTIC collision -- not a coincidental hash collision, and not required to be
// one. Same domain + same description text always produces the same logical ID.
//
// The colliding description text here (`"Standard attack done with a melee weapon"`)
// is deliberately reused from domains/domain_field_accepts_valid_member.gddl, whose
// `melee_weapon` entry under this same `ActionAttack` domain already has an
// independently-verified logical ID for this exact domain-qualified string:
// `5c96a731d7d47e03` (confirmed via a from-scratch FNV-1a-64 cross-check earlier in
// this corpus's history, not just taken on trust). Reusing it here means this
// fixture's predicted shared hash value is itself already independently verified,
// not a fresh, unconfirmed computation.
//
// PHASE/CHECK: spec-grounded, not a guess. GDDL Spec v4 §4.1 (Collision Detection
// subsection) explicitly states this is caught at phase 4 (Register). The check name
// (`id_collision`) was specified directly in the original request to Compiler Core,
// not inferred. Still worth Compiler Core's actual run confirming it lands exactly
// there in practice -- same as any other prediction in this corpus -- but this is not
// the same category of genuinely-undetermined-until-implementation-runs question that,
// e.g., the bare-scalar-field phase question originally was before it got resolved.

identifier ActionAttack
	melee_weapon = "Standard attack done with a melee weapon"
	heavy_strike = "Standard attack done with a melee weapon"
	// COLLISION: identical description text to `melee_weapon` above, different key.
	// Both entries hash to the same logical ID under this domain, deterministically.

